STEP 2 -- forwarding granularity: per-packet or per-flow?                        PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Guide authority, same section, verbatim:
  > "On AlliedWare Plus switches, ECMP calculations are done on a per-packet basis.
  >  This means that the packets in a flow may take different paths."

  If that holds, the packets of ONE flow must split. Decisive leg: 1 flow, 400
  frames.

  leg L3-single-flow: --flows 1 --per-flow 400 --vary dst
  counter        before    after    delta   minus drift (~52 over 33 s)  => TEST
  swi_d p1.0.4   2715      3167     +452                                 ~400
  swi_d p1.0.16  3450      3501     +51                                  ~0

  => STEP 2 PASS: all 400 packets of a single flow took the SAME path, with the
     other port proven forwarding in the same minute by the legs either side of
     this one. ECMP on this x960-56YSQ is therefore PER-FLOW -- a header hash --
     and NOT per-packet, which is the opposite of what the guide states for
     AlliedWare Plus switches. Per-flow is the better behaviour (per-packet
     forwarding reorders TCP), so this reads as a documentation defect rather than
     a product defect, and it is not a case failure: the TPS row is "ECMP routing",
     and ECMP routing works. It is recorded because anyone sizing or debugging ECMP
     from the guide would predict the wrong thing.

STEP 3 -- which header fields the hash uses, and what that means for STEP 1      PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  3a. ONE FIELD VARIED PER LEG, everything else held constant. 64 flows x 20 frames
      each = 1280 test frames per leg.

      leg               swi_d p1.0.4 delta   swi_d p1.0.16 delta   => split (drift ~37/port)
      L4-vary-dst       3199 -> 3875  +676    3532 -> 4210  +678    ~639 / ~641  = 50/50
      L4-vary-src       3886 -> 4562  +676    4221 -> 4897  +676    ~639 / ~639  = 50/50
      L4-vary-sport     4574 -> 5890  +1316   4908 -> 4944  +36     ~1280 / ~0   = NO SPREAD
      L4-vary-dport     5901 -> 7218  +1317   4957 -> 4994  +37     ~1280 / ~0   = NO SPREAD

      The ECMP hash on this platform uses the IPv4 SOURCE and DESTINATION ADDRESSES
      ONLY; the L4 ports are not hash inputs. 64 distinct source ports, or 64
      distinct destination ports, with the addresses held constant, put every one of
      1280 frames on a single egress port. Either address alone spreads 50/50. This
      is normal for switch silicon and is not a defect, but it is not documented in
      the guide, and it matters: a deployment whose flows differ only in L4 port
      (one client to one server, many sessions) gets NO load sharing from ECMP on
      this hardware.

  3b. WHICH EXPLAINS STEP 1's 81 / 19, and corrects it. The STEP 1 flow set varied
      source, destination, source port and destination port ALL BY THE SAME INDEX
      -- flow i was 192.168.10.(100+i) -> 198.51.100.(10+i). With a hash over the
      source and destination addresses only, two addresses incrementing in lockstep
      are strongly correlated and the entropy largely cancels. That is a property of
      the flow set, not of the device. Decorrelating the two addresses -- source
      over 64 values, destination over 7, so they no longer move together:

      leg L5-decorrelated: --flows 64 --per-flow 20 --vary src,dst --src-span 64 --dst-span 7
      counter        before    after    delta   minus drift  => TEST
      swi_d p1.0.4   7272      7948     +676    ~-37         ~639
      swi_d p1.0.16  5048      5725     +677    ~-37         ~640

  => STEP 3 PASS: the hash inputs are identified by measurement, one field at a
     time, each against a control leg in the same minute -- the two IPv4 addresses
     spread traffic 50/50 on their own, and neither L4 port spreads it at all. With
     genuinely independent hash inputs the device distributes evenly, ~639 / ~640 of
     1280 test frames. This DECORRELATED leg is the distribution measurement this
     case rests on; a flow set whose fields move in lockstep is a weak instrument
     and its ratio is not evidence about the device.

STEP 4 -- far-side attribution, read by scapy rather than by a counter           PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Both working host NICs land on the DUT, so there is no scapy host behind a
  partner. The far side is therefore made to speak for itself: 198.51.100.0/24 is
  unrouted ON the partners, so each partner that receives a flow answers with an
  ICMP destination-unreachable SOURCED FROM ITS OWN ADDRESS, which travels back
  through the DUT to eth3 and is decoded there. The DUT's ARP for the single source
  address was primed first with
  `arpleg.py --answer 192.168.10.5=00:11:22:33:44:01`, so the unreachables have
  somewhere to go.

  leg L6-attribution: --flows 64 --per-flow 10 --vary dst --src-base 192.168.10.5
      (640 frames)
    "cap L6-attribution-eth3.pcap: 644 frame(s), 644 of them ours"
    ICMP unreachables by SOURCE (which router received the flow):
      10.0.61.2   code 0   x2        <-- swi_b received flows
      10.0.41.2   code 0   x2        <-- swi_a received flows

  counter        before    after    delta   minus drift  => TEST
  swi_d p1.0.4   7977      8329     +352    ~-27         ~325
  swi_d p1.0.16  5753      6106     +353    ~-27         ~326

  => STEP 4 PASS: both next hops answered, each from its own address, in the SAME
     leg -- far-side confirmation of the split that does not depend on any device
     counter, while the DUT's own egress counters show the same flow set divided
     ~325 / ~326. Only 2 unreachables came back per router because ICMP unreachable
     generation is rate-limited on these platforms, so this attribution is
     qualitative -- WHICH routers received traffic -- and the counters carry the
     quantity.

